So `getNextDataLine` is called. Let me look at `getNextDataLine` more carefully:

```cpp
bool IOStreamBuffer<T>::getNextDataLine(std::vector<T> &buffer, T continuationToken) {
    buffer.resize(m_cacheSize);  // buffer has m_cacheSize elements
    if (m_cachePos >= m_cacheSize || 0 == m_filePos) {
        if (!readNextBlock()) return false;
    }

    bool continuationFound(false);
    size_t i = 0;
    for(;;) {
        if (continuationToken == m_cache[m_cachePos]) {
            continuationFound = true;
            ++m_cachePos;
        }
        if (IsLineEnd(m_cache[m_cachePos])) {
            if (!continuationFound) {
                break;  // end of data line
            } else {
                // skip line end
                while (m_cache[m_cachePos] != '\n') ++m_cachePos;
                ++m_cachePos;
                continuationFound = false;
            }
        }

        buffer[i] = m_cache[m_cachePos];  // <-- OOB write when i >= buffer.size()
        ++m_cachePos;
        ++i;
        if (m_cachePos >= size()) break;
        if (m_cachePos >= m_cacheSize) {
            if (!readNextBlock()) return false;
        }
    }

    buffer[i] = '\n';  // <-- OOB write when i >= buffer.size()
    ++m_cachePos;
    return true;
}
```

Wait, `m_cachePos >= size()` check at line 281. `size()` returns `m_filesize`. So if `m_cachePos >= m_filesize`, it breaks. But `m_cachePos` is the position within the cache, not the file. Let me re-check.

Actually, `m_cachePos` is the position within the current cache block. `m_filePos` is the file position. So `m_cachePos >= size()` compares the cache position against the file size, which doesn't make sense... unless `size()` is supposed to be `m_cacheSize`.

Wait, `size()` returns `m_filesize`. So `m_cachePos >= m_filesize` would break. But `m_cachePos` is the position within the cache, which is at most `m_cacheSize`. For a 20MB file with 16MB cache, `m_cacheSize = 16MB` and `m_filesize = 20MB`. So `m_cachePos` (max 16MB) would never be >= `m_filesize` (20MB). So the check at line 281 doesn't help.

But wait, `m_cachePos` is reset to 0 after each `readNextBlock()`. So `m_cachePos` goes from 0 to `m_cacheSize` (16MB), then `readNextBlock()` is called, and `m_cachePos` resets to 0. So `m_cachePos >= size()` (16MB >= 20MB) is false, and the loop continues.

But `i` keeps growing! After the first block, `i = 16MB`. After the second block, `i` goes from 16MB to 20MB. But `buffer` was resized to 16MB. So `buffer[16MB]` is an OOB write.

